Q: Does the C99 standard allow anonymous enums? A: <p>“Anonymous structure” in the C standard refers to a structure inside another structure that does not have a member name. Not having a tag is a different matter.</p>
<p>The C standard has always provide for a declaration of a structure, union, or enumeration type not to have a tag. This can be seen in C 1999 6.7.2.1 and 6.7.2.2, where part of the grammar is:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><code>enum</code> <em>identifier</em><sub>opt</sub> <code>{</code> <em>enumerator-list</em> <code>}</code></p>
</blockquote>
<p>The “opt” subscript means that item is optional.</p>
<p>Consider the declaration <code>struct foo { int i; } s;</code>. In this declaration, <code>s</code> is the name of the structure (the object in memory). <code>foo</code> is not the name of the structure. It is a part of a name (<code>struct foo</code>) for the type.</p>
<p>C 2011 introduced anonymous structures and unions. What changed is that the name of a structure or union member inside another structure could be omitted. This is what makes the structure anonymous (without name); its name is omitted.</p>
<p>This is an example of an anonymous structure:</p>
<pre><code>struct foo
{
    int i;
    struct
    {
        int a;
        int b;
    } /* No member name here. */ ;
} x;
</code></pre>
<p>With this, the type <code>struct foo</code> and the object <code>x</code> have a <code>struct bar</code> inside them, but there is no name for that whole member. The members within <code>struct bar</code> can be accessed by the names <code>x.a</code> and <code>x.b</code>, but there is no name <code>x.something</code> that would refer to the <code>struct bar</code>. That is what makes the structure anonymous. (Its type still has a name, <code>struct bar</code>, which has the tag <code>bar</code>.)</p>
<p>The part of the grammar in C 2024 that allows anonymous structures or unions is the part that says a <em>member-declaration</em> may be “<em>attribute-specifier-sequence</em><sub>opt</sub> <em>specifier-qualifier-list</em> <em>member-declarator-list</em><sub>opt</sub> <code>;</code>” (C 2024 6.7.3.2). It is that subscript “opt” on the <em>member-declarator-list</em> that says there might be no member name. In the Constraints section, this clause says “A member declaration that does not declare an anonymous structure or anonymous union shall contain a member declarator list.” That means the “opt” applies only for anonymous structures or unions—if this member of the structure is anything else, such as an <code>enum</code>, then it must have a member declarator list, so a member name must be supplied. (Except that a bit-field can be declared with no member name, just a width, due to further complications in the grammar not discussed here.)</p>
<p>That settles C 2024. In C 1999, since there are no anonymous structures or unions, there is no corresponding “opt” in the grammar. The corresponding part of the C 1999 grammar, in 6.7.2.1, says a <em>struct-declaration</em> may be “<em>specifier-qualifier-list</em> <em>struct-declarator-list</em> <code>;</code>”. The ”<em>struct-declarator-list</em>” is not optional, so a member name is required (again except for a bit-field).</p>
<p>Thus, the C 1999 grammar does not admit an anonymous <code>enum</code>. (Nonetheless, the standard permits a C implementation to extend the language by accepting an anonymous <code>enum</code>.) However, this would seem to be useless. Anonymous structures are unions are useful because their parts can be referred to by name because those names are injected in the name space of the containing structure. (In the above example, the <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> in <code>struct bar</code> can be referred to as <code>x.a</code> and <code>x.b</code>.) This also means the names are simpler than if the structure or union were not anonymous (<code>x.a</code> is shorter than <code>x.something.a</code>). This would not work with an anonymous enumeration. The names for an enumeration are names for values, not names for member objects inside the enumeration. So, if we had a structure with an anonymous enumeration, there would be no name that refers to the enumeration. For example, with:</p>
<pre><code>struct foo
{
    int i;
    enum bar
    {
        a,
        b,
    } /* No member name here. */ ;
} x;
</code></pre>
<p>there is no <code>something</code> for which we can say <code>x.something = b;</code> to set the value of the enumeration member.</p>
